description.text:
Collectors from around the world gathered in Shelbyville, Kentucky, as dozens of personal belongings once owned by KFC founder Colonel Harland Sanders went under the hammer. Organized by The Menish Group, the auction featured more than 60 items spanning Sanders' personal life and business career, attracting bidders eager to own a piece of fast-food history. Among the most anticipated items was Sanders' signature black clip-on bow tie, one of the defining elements of the image that helped transform Kentucky Fried Chicken into a globally recognized brand. The bow tie sold for 8,000 dollars, while Sanders' personal planner from 1964 became the highest-selling item of the evening, fetching 30,000 dollars. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

Other memorabilia included his original cash register, engraved pressure cookers, carving knives, watches, business documents, and a Kentucky Colonel certificate. The collection also featured personal correspondence, photographs, and household items preserved by a family that acquired them directly from Sanders decades ago.

Auction organizers described the sale as one of the most significant collections of Colonel Sanders memorabilia ever offered publicly, reflecting the enduring fascination with the entrepreneur who became one of the world's earliest global restaurant icons.
